<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mahinda (Buddhist monk)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">3rd century BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mahinda was a Buddhist monk, said to be a son of the Maurya emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashoka">Ashoka</a>, who brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> in the 3rd century BCE. According to the chronicles he converted King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/devanampiya-tissa">Devanampiya Tissa</a> at Mihintale, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahavihara">Mahavihara</a> monastery was founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anuradhapura">Anuradhapura</a> soon after. He is venerated in Sri Lanka as the founder of the island's Buddhist tradition.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mahinda-buddhist-monk.jpg" alt="Mahinda (Buddhist monk)" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MediaJet · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Arhat_Mahinda_Thera_(Son_of_Emperor_Ashoka).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
